> Checked inputs and owned evidence

The original four-argument function still returns the boundary-inclusive vector. The new
finite_difference_poisson_solve returns those same numerical values plus an owned snapshot
of the actual grid, samples, RHS and residuals. It evaluates the source exactly once per
interior node, in increasing coordinate order. Neither function samples the endpoints.
Source exceptions propagate unchanged. Callback side effects already performed cannot
be undone if a later sample fails.

Both APIs require n>0, a nonempty callback and finite boundary/source values. The legacy
signed count n+2 must fit int; impossible signed grids are rejected before allocation
or callbacks. Finite-input arithmetic overflow also raises an exception. The ordinary
solve has O(n) storage; the diagnostic result has additional O(n) numeric arrays.
These are outputs describing one solve, without an optional iteration-history quota.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fd_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <climits>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
int callbacks=0;const auto f=[&](double){++callbacks;return 1.;};
  int rejected=0;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::finite_difference_poisson(f,0,0,0);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::finite_difference_poisson({},0,0,1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::finite_difference_poisson(f,std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity(),0,1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::finite_difference_poisson(f,0,0,INT_MAX);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  std::print("Invalid zero grid, missing source, nonfinite boundary and oversized grid: {}/4 rejected; callbacks {}.\n",rejected,callbacks);
  const auto source=[&](double x){++callbacks;return x;};auto r=dm::algorithms::finite_difference_poisson_solve(source,0,0,3);const auto kept=r;
  r.x[1]=99;r.source[0]=99;r.rhs[0]=99;r.u[1]=99;r.residual[0]=99;
  std::print("Successful n=3 solve: callbacks {}; retained source[0] {}; retained U[1] {:.8f}; later source and U edits {}.\n",callbacks,kept.source[0],kept.u[1],r.u[1]);
  const auto ordinary=dm::algorithms::finite_difference_poisson([](double x){return x;},0,0,3);
  std::print("Legacy vector equals retained diagnostic values: {}.\n",ordinary==kept.u);
  dm::graphics::Diagram g(60,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle b;b.width=3.4;b.height=2;b.font_size=23;
  g.node("callback",{0,0},"Source callback\n3 evaluations\nno endpoint calls",b).node("solve",{5,0},"Native assembly\nThomas solve\nfinite checks",b).node("result",{10,0},"Owned snapshot\nx, source, RHS\nU and residual",b).edge("callback","solve").edge("solve","result").text({5,-2.5},"Copying the result preserves every numerical array",25).save_svg("build/fd_controls.svg");
  if(rejected!=4||callbacks!=3||ordinary!=kept.u)throw std::runtime_error("checked controls");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Invalid zero grid, missing source, nonfinite boundary and oversized grid: 4/4 rejected; callbacks 0.
Successful n=3 solve: callbacks 3; retained source[0] 0.25; retained U[1] 0.03906250; later source and U edits 99.
Legacy vector equals retained diagnostic values: true.
\image(figures/fd_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Input checks precede source calls. A successful three-interior-node solve calls the source three times; an owned copy survives later array edits and agrees with the ordinary vector API.)
\alttext(A native three-stage diagram shows three source evaluations, assembly and Thomas solution, and an owned snapshot containing grid, samples, right-hand side, nodal values and residuals.)

> What these checks establish

The original smooth sine example shows second-order nodal convergence, supported by a
closed-form discrete eigenmode prediction. Tiny systems expose the boundary signs and
the one-row case. Polynomial references show why exact nodes and an exact continuous
field are different claims. Frequency controls show that a small linear-system residual
can coexist with a large discretisation error. The API validates its numerical domain;
it does not certify source smoothness, conditioning, a continuous error bound, or the
adequacy of a chosen grid for a particular application.

> References

- P.-O. Persson, [Finite Difference Methods for PDEs](https://persson.berkeley.edu/math228b/html/finite_difference_methods_for_PDEs.html), sections Boundary Value Problems, Local Truncation Error, Stability and Convergence. The notes write u''=f; this report and the native API use −u''=f. The stencil, stability and eigenmode analysis are adjusted consistently for that sign.
- I. J. Sobey, [Finite Differences](https://info.sjc.ox.ac.uk/scr/sobey/iblt/chapter1/notes/node4.html), equations 12–18 and Note 4. The native implementation here fixes the interval and uses uniform Dirichlet nodes; it does not implement those notes' other boundary conditions or time-dependent extensions.

